{"plan_objective_id": 1, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 1,
  "objective": "Research sieve cleaning methods per ASTM/ISO standards and sieve-maker guidance",
  "rationale": "Need to understand standard cleaning methods for test sieves.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found comprehensive guidance from sieve-maker handbooks (W.S. Tyler, Advantech), NIST guidance (Dapkunas 2001), and Neikov handbook. Key findings: ultrasonic cleaning in mild detergent/water recommended; brushing with nylon bristle brush for mesh ≥100 only; no brushing for <100 mesh; no sharp objects; no compressed air on fine meshes; max sieve temp 149°C (300°F) for solder joints; epoxy repairs may become brittle.",
  "evaluation": "Multiple sources confirm consistent recommendations. Specific ultrasonic frequency/temperature/duration not specified in standards but general principles well-established."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 2, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 2,
  "objective": "Research AM-grade powder handling standards regarding cross-contamination and dedicated sieves",
  "rationale": "Need to determine whether cleaning alone suffices or dedicated sieves per alloy family are required.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found: ISO/ASTM 52907 addresses powder characterization/quality but no specific cleaning standardization exists. ISO/ASTM 52928 recommends assigning containers to single alloy base. NASA requires dedicated machines per alloy. Brandão (2017) recommends one powder type per AM equipment for critical applications. No standards prescribe specific sieve-cleanliness verification methods. Detection methods: SEM/EDS, XCT, ICP-OES depending on material pair.",
  "evaluation": "Strong consensus that dedicated equipment is best practice for critical applications. No formal standard prescribes sieve-cleaning verification protocols specifically."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 3, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 3,
  "objective": "Research Cu-Sn contamination effects in aluminum LPBF powder and cross-contamination cases",
  "rationale": "Need documented cases of foreign-alloy particle contamination.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found: Horn et al. (2019) studied CuCr1Zr in AlSi10Mg at 0.5-5 wt% - Cu-rich inclusions, Al2Cu formation, embrittlement, reduced elongation. Alexopoulos et al. (2026) confirmed yield stress increases, elongation decreases linearly. Brandão (2017) documented W in Ti-6Al-4V causing scatter and reduced elongation. Balart (2021) found NiCrCuP in 316L causing microcracking. Atkins (2026) found Ti-6Al-4V and 316L in AlSi10Mg causing scratches. Santecchia (2019) quantified Ti64/maraging steel cross-contamination via SEM/EDS.",
  "evaluation": "Comprehensive documentation of Cu-alloy contamination in aluminum LPBF at multiple levels. Direct relevance to the user's Cu-Sn → Al scenario."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 4, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 4,
  "objective": "Research safety hazards: bronze-aluminum thermite risk, combustible dust, wet cleaning hydrogen",
  "rationale": "Need to address thermite risks, hydrogen generation, solvent choice.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found: Cadwallader (2003) documented Al + CuO thermite incident - 50g ignited during mixing. Al dust is Hazard Class 3, Kst >300. Zhang (2020) documented hydrogen generation from Al+water in wet dust systems, 2Al+6H2O→2Al(OH)3+3H2, theoretical 3.7 wt% H2. Reding (2018) reviewed NFPA 484 for combustible metals. Groarke (2021) recommends IPA wipes rather than water for Al powder cleaning. Small quantities in sieve cleaning pose low but non-zero risk requiring ventilation.",
  "evaluation": "Thermite risk from CuO+Al documented. Hydrogen risk from wet cleaning is real but manageable with small quantities, ventilation, and proper procedures."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 5, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 5,
  "objective": "Compile step-by-step cleaning and verification procedure for university lab",
  "rationale": "Need to synthesize all findings into a practical procedure.",
  "status": "in-progress",
  "result": null,
  "evaluation": null
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 5, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 5,
  "objective": "Compile step-by-step cleaning and verification procedure for university lab",
  "rationale": "Need to synthesize all findings into a practical procedure.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Created detailed 15-step procedure artifact covering safety prep, dry recovery, brushing (250 μm only), ultrasonic cleaning, rinsing, drying, inspection, residue screening, sacrificial flush, analysis, release/quarantine, dedicated sieves recommendation, and post-Al cleaning safety.",
  "evaluation": "Comprehensive procedure that distinguishes between standards-backed, vendor-guided, and common-practice recommendations."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 6, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 6,
  "objective": "Submit final answer with all artifacts embedded",
  "rationale": "All objectives completed, need to compile and submit the comprehensive answer.",
  "status": "in-progress",
  "result": null,
  "evaluation": null
}"}

